    url = "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0003496725017303",
    keywords = "Epidemiology, Cardiovascular system",
    abstract = "Background: Sarcoidosis, characterized by non-necrotizing granulomatous inflammation, is a multisystem disease with an unpredictable disease spectrum. An estimated 5\% of sarcoidosis patients have clinically evident cardiac involvement while 25\% may have asymptomatic silent disease leading to challenging diagnosis. Objectives: We aim to study the clinical characteristics in a cohort of cardiac sarcoidosis patients to identify patterns of cardiac involvement and clinical correlations. Methods: We conducted a single center retrospective study of patients with cardiac sarcoidosis at Mayo Clinic Florida. The study was approved by the Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board (IRB). Inclusion criteria was patients older than 18 years of age, with diagnosis of sarcoid myocarditis/cardiac sarcoidosis/sarcoid heart muscle disease specified ICD-10 code D86.85 with established visits using specified CPT codes between 02/25/2018 till 08/24/2023. A list of 99 patients was generated and 76 patients met above criteria. Subsequently data from each patient was collected that included baseline demographics and disease characteristics. Results: Table 1 provides patient demographics. 44 (57.9\%) patients were male and 32 (42.1 \%) were female with 59 (77.6\%) Caucasian and 14 (18.4\%) African American. There were 7 deaths (9.2\% mortality) with 1 from cardiogenic shock, 1 cardiopulmonary arrest and 1 attributed to side effects of immunosuppressive therapy. 13 (17.1\%) patients had a positive exposure history to chemicals and toxins. Only 6 (17.6\%) patients had an abnormal angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) level. Non-necrotizing granulomas noted on pathology in 19 (25.0\%) patients and 54 (71.1\%) had negative biopsy. Tissue sampled included lung 15 (19.7\%), lymph node 19 (25.0\%) and cardiac 10 (13.2\%). 49 (64.5\%) patients had cardiomyopathy with dilated being the most common and heart failure reduced ejection fraction (EF) the predominant type (N=28[41.8\%]). 27 (35.5\%) patients had abnormal electrocardiogram (EKG) with premature ventricular contractions (PVCs), atrial fibrillation and atrioventricular (AV) blocks the most frequent rhythm abnormalities. Cardiac MRI was performed in 60 cases and 55 (91.7\%) had late gadolinium enhancement on magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). (PET) was performed in 47 cases and was positive in 42 (89.4\%) cases. 69 (90.8\%) patients achieved remission while relapsing disease was an unfortunate occurrence in 36 (47.4\%) cases [Figure 1]. Conclusion: Development of a standardized diagnostic algorithm and classification criteria for cardiac sarcoidosis is imperative. Our cohort recorded a high mortality and relapse rate and surveillance for cardiac involvement must be undertaken in all patients with sarcoidosis. Certain occupational exposures were identified and prompts raising awareness for environmental safety. Figure 1Relapse probability over time (months) REFERENCES: NIL. Table 1Total (N=76)Gender, n (\%)Female32 (42.1\%)Male44 (57.9\%)Race, n (\%)African American14 (18.4\%)American Indian/ Alaskan Native1 (1.3\%)Asian1 (1.3\%)Caribbean black1 (1.3\%)White59 (77.6\%)Ethnicity, n (\%)Hispanic or Latino2 (2.6\%)Not Hispanic or Latino74 (97.4\%)Death, n (\%)No69 (90.8\%)Yes7 (9.2\%)Cause of death, n (\%)n=7Cardiogenic shock/Heart failure secondary to Cardiac sarcoidosis1 (33.3\%)Cardiopulmonary arrest1 (33.3\%)Side effects of Humira1 (33.3\%)Sarcoidosis other organ involvement, n (\%)n=73Negative50 (68.5\%)Pulmonary18 (24.7\%)Skin1 (1.4\%)Neurosarcoidosis1 (1.4\%)Hepatic1 (1.4\%)Neurosarcoidosis1 (1.4\%)Bone1 (1.4\%)Smoking status, n (\%)N=74Never64 (86.5\%)Former9 (12.2\%)Current1 (1.4\%)Occupational/Environmental exposures, n (\%)No63 (82.9\%)Yes13 (17.1\%)Exposure type, n (\%)n=13Asbestos1 (7.7\%)Unknown1 (7.7\%)Ground Zero2 (15.4\%)Roofing1 (7.7\%)Meat packing plant1 (7.7\%)Military equipment3 (23.1\%)Organophosphate/ Toxic paint fumes1 (7.7\%)Pesticides/mold1 (7.7\%)Radiation1 (7.7\%)Beeke